    "## Mathematical Formulation From Scratch for Multiple Linear Regression\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Types of Linear Regression:\n",
    "- Simple\n",
    "- Multiple\n",
    "- Polynomial\n",
    "- Ridge\n",
    "- Lasso\n",
    "- Elastic Net\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Simple Linear Regression**: Uses exactly one independent variable to predict a dependent variable (e.g., predicting salary based strictly on years of experience). The formula is \\(y = mx + c\\).\n",
    "2. **Multiple Linear Regression**: Uses two or more independent variables to predict a single dependent variable (e.g., predicting house prices using area, bedrooms, and age). The formula is \\(y = m_1x_1 + m_2x_2 + \\dots + mx + c\\).\n",
    "3. **Polynomial Linear Regression**:Used when data shows a non-linear (curved) relationship. It fits a curved line by adding polynomial powers of the independent variable (e.g., \\(y = m_1x + m_2x^2 + c\\)).\n",
    "4. **Ridge Regression**: A regularization technique that penalizes large coefficients, effectively reducing model complexity and preventing overfitting, especially when independent variables are highly correlated.\n",
    "5. **Lasso Regression**: Similar to Ridge, it adds a penalty for larger coefficients but can shrink less important feature coefficients all the way to zero, functioning as automatic feature selection.\n",
    "6. **Elastic Net Regression**: Combines the penalty properties of both Ridge and Lasso regularization to optimize models with highly complex and correlated datasets."
   ]
